    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the difference between a retest date and an expiry date?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A retest date is the date by which an active pharmaceutical ingredient should be sampled and tested again against its specification; if it still conforms, it may be used for a further defined period. An expiry date is final: after it, the material should not be used, whatever a test would show."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can an API be used after its retest date has passed?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Only after it has been sampled and tested again against the specification it was released to, and found to conform. Until that testing is complete the material is not available for manufacture. If it fails, it is handled as out of specification material under your own procedure, not reassigned to a product thought to be less demanding."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How many times can the same batch be retested?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "That comes from the manufacturer's stability data and from your own written procedure, not from repeating the interval indefinitely. Each retest confirms conformity at that moment; it does not make the material new. Decide the total permitted in-use period and the number of retests allowed before the first date approaches, and record the reasoning behind both."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does the API retest date set my finished product's expiry date?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. Your product's shelf life comes from stability studies on your own formulation in its own packaging. The API date governs only when the starting material may still be taken into manufacture. Using material late in its permitted period does not shorten your product's approved shelf life, but it leaves you less room if anything delays the batch."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What if the certificate of analysis gives neither a retest nor an expiry date?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Ask the supplier which one applies and on what basis, and ask for the stability summary behind it. A certificate with no such date, or with vague wording like valid until, leaves the receiving site guessing about an obligation that ought to be explicit. Have it corrected at source rather than resolving the ambiguity locally."
      }
    }
  ]
